#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175634 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "7498df7d-a340-599e-b4ba-496f5142c28c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12379" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the cone $z=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ that is subject to the constraints  $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 4,\\, x\\geq 0$ and $y\\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad (x,y)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x^2+y^2\\leq 4, \\, x\\geq 0, \\, y\\geq 0 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/be8246dc3bfe1bf16af5d9913fc4e6aef58ddad282646a00d3e31120f6bf31e8.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175751 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/id #uuid "bcdc7b9f-b5f8-5516-a0e6-8ca54fb80a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-8948" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $x =y+\\sqrt 2 z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by the constraints $1 \\leq y\\leq 2$ and $1 \\leq z \\leq 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial y}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial z}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx = y+\\sqrt 2 z, \\qquad (y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 1 \\leq z \\leq 3\\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/240ac546b0dd725e392dd0bd475b2ef00eebce2594be5921285e227137538c46.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+\\sqrt{2}z)=1, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y+\\sqrt{2}z)=\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(1)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175752 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "80c2abda-a1d9-569e-936e-fc44ba77eb50" :question/math-academy-id "e-8959" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z = y - 4x$ that is bounded by the constraints $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z = f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = y - 4x, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-4x)=-4, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-4x)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-4)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+16+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{18}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175770 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "77e8fb3e-1858-5831-b740-a0c133c8180f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9141" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the plane $z = 2y+2x$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\cos{x}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz = 2y+2x, \\qquad (x,y)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\cos{x} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/e74d609a1288446f0f3adc0de65b814f2a6daaf2d398478e7834882f3be68c8c.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y+2x)=2, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y+2x)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3(1-0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1b43b1a-1d4f-5e1d-b50b-29e40a4f4372" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(π\\sqrt{π} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173479} {:answer/id #uuid "c2940e97-59ad-57d0-96ce-e6ecc4cb22c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173480} {:answer/id #uuid "18697c0c-2948-5dbd-a93f-274a2fc87324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{2} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173481} {:answer/id #uuid "29d42868-da7f-5103-862d-04bb0580ab84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173482} {:answer/id #uuid "58a21276-f065-5541-9ce2-4051a2c0741a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3} - 2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58a21276-f065-5541-9ce2-4051a2c0741a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3} - 2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173483} :answer-field/id #uuid "f987312d-9a9b-5513-8911-d05b339c27a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40e44b1f-1930-5622-81b2-291ea09677cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-106034" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z$ whose projection onto the $xz$-plane is bounded by $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and the $x$-axis for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z,\\,(x,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/be5dc3bf3ed4b6b7bee06d583b69e40a3922edede1e61a6f0b68d2ce0767f92a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=2+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\sqrt{2+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{3}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({3}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(3\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af81d187-390f-52a8-b640-67ff829dba62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173647} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a6aa28-4251-574a-9e9c-9dd91a4e6a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173648} {:answer/id #uuid "c767c49a-114b-5d17-8f38-a90bceee4fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173649} {:answer/id #uuid "f8201991-cdf5-5fa6-8942-7709c6a1f656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173650} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8981db-1845-561b-a922-4d0dd187f635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5a6aa28-4251-574a-9e9c-9dd91a4e6a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173648} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7b7fcec-d74f-5573-98b7-5f2e845cdfc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d9b2c67-e61f-5dae-a147-dc5eaf3565f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-109594" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z=2x+3y$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=2x+3y,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+3y)=2, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+3y)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4+9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8abbdf4-19fd-51f5-b473-e1af1dc33c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}1 + x^{2} + y^{2}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173653} {:answer/id #uuid "e9acb167-2737-5291-b7b1-248245a96c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1 + x + y}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173654} {:answer/id #uuid "1bebd994-6a12-5b90-9430-60b042c9aaa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{2} + 4y^{2}}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173655} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf61d90-9f9d-59a7-b655-e97ed6fc23bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1 + 2x + 2y}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173656} {:answer/id #uuid "4f49bcd8-6138-581d-926e-bdbb06a64428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1 + 2x^{2} + 2y^{2}}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bebd994-6a12-5b90-9430-60b042c9aaa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{2} + 4y^{2}}dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173655} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c77966-d76d-5dda-b825-f36361203c91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c85ae37-614c-52e2-a347-0d9aae387c55" :question/math-academy-id "q-109600" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by the lines $y=x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/0b6de7829e702377d1b20bb1b297d3d28e6fb8bae9672b0137d92b4d53a96c83.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70adfa2d-4b67-584c-9484-97c32800a4ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352695} {:answer/id #uuid "11248fc3-e0c6-5a5b-a72c-5dd596495f80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352696} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3cbae4-2acc-5da0-b0ea-95b6888b7c37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352697} {:answer/id #uuid "509f76ce-102a-5af2-95df-16f2d47e8cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352698} {:answer/id #uuid "d8092ac4-a60f-5cde-937e-06dc44208de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8092ac4-a60f-5cde-937e-06dc44208de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352699} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d487cd-4f73-5fbf-8719-feeada2b20ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "352914bd-a34c-5653-8a01-902adc8d34d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-161126" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(4y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa8621d1-c419-53dd-b663-00f61eeb51ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+36{x}^{4}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352702} {:answer/id #uuid "a298a43a-de4a-5939-bc89-64e2653b2843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3{x}^{2}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352703} {:answer/id #uuid "71a08eae-5c23-5d88-8231-c30d64229b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}+2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352704} {:answer/id #uuid "be98f77c-179c-589b-ac74-a29353f7e192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352705} {:answer/id #uuid "77fbd9c5-98a2-51a3-b353-6c717fa64cbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+36{x}^{4}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be98f77c-179c-589b-ac74-a29353f7e192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352705} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ac79f44-1490-511a-a698-e5dce7376de0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52890d5c-d18d-50d0-a821-47f1d481ca67" :question/math-academy-id "q-161131" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz={y}^{2}-{x}^{3},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{3})=-3{x}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{3})=2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-3{x}^{2})}^{2}+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{4}+4{y}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe52a835-c6dd-5e14-b206-1109cb89b1b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{4}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352709} {:answer/id #uuid "9606c1a2-c5a6-5e2a-8935-e599d278ce94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+y+xy}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352710} {:answer/id #uuid "d204edd8-ad6b-5521-a688-45bdbc9d2cad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352711} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae820bd-ed2a-568e-9e1b-21ba0328eb67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}+2xy}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352712} {:answer/id #uuid "4a61bc62-0314-55f9-870b-6c7f7c34ddb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}1+{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d204edd8-ad6b-5521-a688-45bdbc9d2cad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352711} :answer-field/id #uuid "6912ebe3-cece-58f0-b087-684927f53e7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c26fb9d1-bd64-5afc-83db-47655a4115bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161138" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the surface $z=x{y}^{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y={x}^{2},$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=x{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/1419b067fe92945249655c5d29498aa4d3b0269d79ebd237fd9ed2a713b70b6a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})={y}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})=2xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{({y}^{2})}^{2}+(2xy)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{4}+4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13fc3982-032f-58af-a621-8d4678e4bfa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352716} {:answer/id #uuid "c4099523-985a-5c47-abb7-2ccdc44b9b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352717} {:answer/id #uuid "5384f711-be91-577f-8e2d-2d1518a1174b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{1+3{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352718} {:answer/id #uuid "7d09cd96-af77-5852-9799-99e6e07e28db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}2+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352719} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1b6292-639c-5e76-b6a2-18a0d7e009eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{1+y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4099523-985a-5c47-abb7-2ccdc44b9b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352717} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab020e3b-88b3-588c-8153-bdca8dced324" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0e01a2d-e5c4-5292-bf63-91ffdef97f70" :question/math-academy-id "q-161139" :question/problem "Consider the portion of the surface $z=x+{y}^{3}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=1.$ Find a repeated integral expression for the surface area of this surface." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=x+{y}^{3},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/87845c65dba220978ca81bd5a2b27816ca15f951cd14d8fe499ab43d3c64ef77.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{3})=1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{3})=3{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(1)^{2}+{(3{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{2+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60eb1ef4-3ee1-5b77-96a3-40ead2033a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352723} {:answer/id #uuid "164d3789-e81b-52bf-a3d3-da038c59e3b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352724} {:answer/id #uuid "9cee0194-c27f-58fc-b48a-2e7f464b9fb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352725} {:answer/id #uuid "6aeee821-ffb1-5ad4-9c8c-0ef8899450d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352726} {:answer/id #uuid "f72135b7-bd58-5062-b07d-a5c307a29aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f72135b7-bd58-5062-b07d-a5c307a29aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352727} :answer-field/id #uuid "d05f83a1-b6ff-587c-bee1-9448ebcaab47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5eba7642-1f97-5622-aff0-b1dcdb8c116f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161144" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the surface $z=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/f6200343142274ee2f5b3934cbe014ee24edf8177f3a544c38dc070bb7df83ef.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}y)=2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{cos}^{2}⁡x+8}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{9(1+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+{cos}^{2}⁡x,$ we get $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\sqrt{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2({2}^{3/2}-1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424fa79f-da14-5580-9c7f-a6b59265e3f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352730} {:answer/id #uuid "5a291b2b-a9c7-52b4-8d07-01c93ee77bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352731} {:answer/id #uuid "51f2d4fb-a1b3-5731-8f67-c21e4a972800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352732} {:answer/id #uuid "1d36de70-4b3f-5019-b1df-8cb76b7f631d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352733} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ebadf-0041-5d43-b38d-74436abc8679" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "424fa79f-da14-5580-9c7f-a6b59265e3f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352730} :answer-field/id #uuid "18828e98-ced4-575f-b972-ee00bb68dc4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c569d995-1da4-5bfa-a879-bcd8318fdf8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161155" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})^{2}+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0f4c9ee-6d8a-5a23-a28e-93f26081a32d" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d30a1ff9-5af3-55ee-b11d-b2bbe48bffa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352737} {:answer/id #uuid "4657dced-7a64-55f2-b150-f584e2a4fbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352738} {:answer/id #uuid "f733e7ea-2507-5aa4-8892-c282c45eca2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352739} {:answer/id #uuid "d6471342-dc82-5870-a5b2-a7dc7a250d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352740} {:answer/id #uuid "b359b3a1-28a9-5b6d-ad9e-02f41fbd3828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4657dced-7a64-55f2-b150-f584e2a4fbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352738} :answer-field/id #uuid "998aa330-474e-58e0-991f-da117ece6ba1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2251dd0b-b951-5753-a1f9-d4dac9a82a77" :question/math-academy-id "q-161156" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the cylinder $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}})^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=\\frac{x}{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}x.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b8cb52-47d0-503d-b890-62b5eea9c0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352744} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd28fd6-6717-53bf-9a31-8c7cf6e15dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352745} {:answer/id #uuid "42840b98-74e2-5729-8ea7-8675405e9120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi (\\sqrt{6}-1)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352746} {:answer/id #uuid "e52cfc04-4b1d-598b-995f-dcc20dec12b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352747} {:answer/id #uuid "ffbf1440-b3be-5a35-b1da-625faeb9258d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e52cfc04-4b1d-598b-995f-dcc20dec12b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352747} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecc5366a-5a8f-5614-9695-de591b0558ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36da176b-60a9-595f-8b40-0f27e3927439" :question/math-academy-id "q-161164" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z=2y-x$ that is subject to the constraints ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9$ and $x\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=2y-x,\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,x\\le 0},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/3c6c556ec90138fa1f0b599c6afed9d093e5c1b10f7751a5fbe55f0add617e1d.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+1+4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{6}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{6}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi \\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2ce0feb-7777-50e3-a5bd-90e7316e52f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (7\\sqrt{7}-1)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352751} {:answer/id #uuid "9050a93e-d934-5669-8559-28f5afa54a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi (3\\sqrt{3}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352752} {:answer/id #uuid "14f02979-da9a-5a04-98c4-4778000d0182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352753} {:answer/id #uuid "c41bb69c-4b78-5c10-8a9d-01963fba070f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (\\sqrt{37}-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352754} {:answer/id #uuid "755df112-be69-53dd-b04f-99816539c6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (\\sqrt{7}-1)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14f02979-da9a-5a04-98c4-4778000d0182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352753} :answer-field/id #uuid "839cb8c3-f449-55f8-bd5e-f1d8f5716366" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5c01955-ec72-53ae-89ff-c2800bbf8d65" :question/math-academy-id "q-161165" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $z=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/d2774aa4a85d25c4e35f3eb327adc82f9ef02eceb2be31ddf730493797e4cac2.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-2x)^{2}+(-2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 3 \\\\ u & 1 & 37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{37}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{37}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{37} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(37)^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (37\\sqrt{37}-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "781b59d5-e6a5-5d74-a468-6219ac8976bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352758} {:answer/id #uuid "2464751c-3502-5a02-baca-f0920b98cf1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (1-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352759} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0047d7-bfbb-562f-a33c-6ab213589ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (2-\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352760} {:answer/id #uuid "db6e7ab3-dfc6-526b-a640-de5bc3bd0ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352761} {:answer/id #uuid "843ffc41-e510-531f-8ab4-c363fda5ae12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (3-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db6e7ab3-dfc6-526b-a640-de5bc3bd0ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352761} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f736d1b-5c23-5447-9377-8fcbdb5fb852" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f453f508-62af-5b81-9c75-095a29efd0bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161167" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that is subject to the constraints ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1$ and $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/e7cb4d16f39d1c1e38d65000471cbc5ca1831a0bbc2cfc61c4f03d087d6a5125.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=4-{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r & =-\\pi {\\int }_{4}^{3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{3}^{4}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 2{({u}^{1/2})}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({4}^{1/2}-{3}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60b5e676-c051-58bb-9b51-77c1086edd00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352765} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6e1d42-939c-5d37-bd6d-36efc5e49cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{13}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352766} {:answer/id #uuid "e2077ed0-b4d5-53c6-a7f7-c620ce5b8662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352767} {:answer/id #uuid "05ba2cfd-c137-5e91-ba1d-5fc01a0615fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352768} {:answer/id #uuid "659f0184-8b96-5de3-bc98-f72341a41712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "659f0184-8b96-5de3-bc98-f72341a41712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352769} :answer-field/id #uuid "77910a6c-921d-56cd-9c6d-209378b5691f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4518bd85-5387-56bc-adec-8e46082ebb8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161176" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the hyperbolic paraboloid $y=1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2},\\,(x,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/ab855ef2b9b5363005c14ea37470ed9ecb5853380a301341d20e2cd6095a5d1f.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2})=-2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-{x}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(-2x)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({x}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2966982a-a726-53e7-923f-2ca43760b756" :knowledge-point/title "Changing to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7313076-bbc3-5ae6-b5a7-14f17aed879f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2π(2\\sqrt{13} - 1))/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174565} {:answer/id #uuid "f33e6a53-6568-5829-ae3e-9e1a72d4c36d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π(\\sqrt{15} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174566} {:answer/id #uuid "19a13215-6e95-5e1d-b10b-b55548c00d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π(\\sqrt{15} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174567} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc9c1c7-7f8a-5bbf-94ff-433b8c9857d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(π(\\sqrt{13} - 1))/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174568} {:answer/id #uuid "6cffbc11-9b78-54a3-8139-c156ea6f95db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(π(13\\sqrt{13} - 1))/(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cffbc11-9b78-54a3-8139-c156ea6f95db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(π(13\\sqrt{13} - 1))/(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174569} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d73709e-a701-5ce7-9bf0-ee5229ad1f4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b708a90c-5d84-533d-ba0c-156466010875" :question/math-academy-id "q-161178" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the paraboloid $x=5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ that is subject to the constraint ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx=5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2},\\,(y,z)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 3},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/66b05919a7644818eea7265ff3ae8fc3ced4ac7d1d6d30352f612649393c876a.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(5+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=2z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2y)^{2}+(2z)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region $D$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+4{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=8r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ u & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{13}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{13}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(13)^{3/2}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (13\\sqrt{13}-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c0f4b8-0b77-5e8a-aae2-2d27ea125d82" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as Z=F(X,Y): Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6ca437-2c92-5e8c-ac6a-348dbf636110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352772} {:answer/id #uuid "7b610b8c-1a13-5f6a-a40a-22f9dad26e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352773} {:answer/id #uuid "7070b859-eba5-56d6-81a1-5cb199364789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352774} {:answer/id #uuid "8079e995-762b-54f2-89cf-169ccd1b1f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352775} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5ee948-d716-50db-8704-3cb99fc6cb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7070b859-eba5-56d6-81a1-5cb199364789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352774} :answer-field/id #uuid "012814c0-611d-509b-98e8-1d5b205b4ec7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e885bc24-125b-51f1-be0b-b6d230809b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161184" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the portion of the surface $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}$ whose projection onto the $xy$-plane is bounded by $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2},\\,(x,y)\\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/b9504140a4827cd74b74e8c23f76e1305389f1bc93a87834a37d4b20222e56f0.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(x)^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c02537e7-f23d-5ad0-997c-a276787fac17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352779} {:answer/id #uuid "b64b4336-74bf-5bf8-9125-6c9898a23f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352780} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0d2795-c1a3-528a-a63a-3ad8dc12d636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352781} {:answer/id #uuid "55f360fa-a32f-52cd-b429-9ed2837a0d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352782} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec1dcab-0684-54f8-b4f2-6822ef83732d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0d2795-c1a3-528a-a63a-3ad8dc12d636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352781} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6ee7bef-542a-5dcf-8a77-4fbe6d9b2eb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9bc498fc-2992-50fe-8096-df2abc6f57f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-161213" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $x={y}^{2}+\\sqrt{3}z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by $z=y,$ $y=1,$ and the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={y}^{2}+\\sqrt{3}z,\\,(y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/0cf571155f2a4cd78d83c4808326cd8102badb3c22f43e00d95aee2494859f48.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\sqrt{3}z)=2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+\\sqrt{3}z)=\\sqrt{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2y)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=1+{y}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=2y\\,\\text{d}y.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({2}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb91da2b-f51d-5774-a7e0-86d658da95c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e+2}-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352786} {:answer/id #uuid "754a4631-3a1f-506d-ae66-8314a4a550e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(e+2)^{3/2}+9\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352787} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b1b74e-eca4-5e1f-a7de-e68b9788858e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3\\sqrt{3}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352788} {:answer/id #uuid "43918ec9-5ef2-5cc2-9671-29203784473c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(e-1)^{3/2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352789} {:answer/id #uuid "7356a9d9-e023-5204-8bcb-e8761f9d8c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(e+2)^{3/2}-3\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7356a9d9-e023-5204-8bcb-e8761f9d8c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(e+2)^{3/2}-3\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352790} :answer-field/id #uuid "f99c02c6-3008-57d5-8018-eba0452dce58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb7c50d7-7f31-5b23-9d88-d62a8dd861b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161214" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the part of the surface $x={e}^{y}-z$ whose projection onto the $yz$-plane is bounded by $z={e}^{2y},$ $y=0,$ $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y}-z,\\,(y,z)\\in D,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le z\\le {e}^{2y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066535/assets/9cae5b68f77c2d79df8a81d0421146ac2bbe618c6065a457c33c86caceeb1327.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}-z)={e}^{y}, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{y}-z)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+({e}^{y})^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{2y}}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot z\\,{|}_{z=0}^{z={e}^{2y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u={e}^{2y}+2,$ we get $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & 0 & 1/2 \\\\ u & 3 & e+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2y}+2}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{3}^{e+2}\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{3}^{e+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((e+2)^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(e+2)^{3/2}-3\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d21f2c-913f-535f-b3b3-45f7aa932a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{2}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352793} {:answer/id #uuid "a534416e-2ec3-53c7-8423-18959d781796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2{y}^{4}+{e}^{z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352794} {:answer/id #uuid "86c95757-b5d0-555b-89c7-7c8cbe99b053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352795} {:answer/id #uuid "80375b1a-abae-52c4-9abf-19ec3ea08b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+{e}^{z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352796} {:answer/id #uuid "caf75422-2788-58e4-93fe-aba0b12689f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+{e}^{2z}-1}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86c95757-b5d0-555b-89c7-7c8cbe99b053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352795} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ed312c5-b769-5c42-9848-b1307a5424b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "528b3e83-41c0-5efb-a577-4265eb93ac16" :question/math-academy-id "q-161230" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(y,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the surface $x={y}^{3}+{e}^{z}$ that lies inside the elliptical cylinder $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={y}^{3}+{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}+{e}^{z})=3{y}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{3}+{e}^{z})={e}^{z},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂x}{∂y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2})}^{2}+({e}^{z})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2dfd0a7-f9f0-5f64-b1bc-6788ff864113" :knowledge-point/title "Surface Areas of Surfaces Defined as X=F(Y,Z) or Y=F(X,Z)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95a93f62-783d-5f91-9dda-70103da50dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{xz}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352800} {:answer/id #uuid "1faf39be-e5cd-551e-88d7-25d3b3fbe22d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352801} {:answer/id #uuid "188ab2a2-54ea-5579-9c49-50616578c24b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x+z}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352802} {:answer/id #uuid "72d48091-8d39-5336-af4d-95a64de51b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{x+z}{{x}^{2}{z}^{2}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\ge 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352803} {:answer/id #uuid "9b22e4b7-5d01-56cf-9b5c-4d57b7907a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}{xz}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1faf39be-e5cd-551e-88d7-25d3b3fbe22d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352801} :answer-field/id #uuid "53d003f1-52c3-5c16-a553-1c7ce00fae13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49516f7e-9986-5620-a6d8-96d7b448f56b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161443" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A,\\,D={(x,z)\\,:\\,\\boxed{}\\,}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the part of the surface $y=\\frac{1}{xz}$ that lies inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$ From left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $y=f(x,z)$ with $(x,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{xz},\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂y}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xz})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}z}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xz})=-\\frac{1}{x{z}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂y}{∂z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}z})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{x{z}^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}{z}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{4}{z}^{4}}}\\,$ and ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 2.$"}]}
